<article class="env-definition" id="010X">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/010X">Definition <span data-tag="010X">12.13.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an additive category. We say a morphism $a : A_\bullet \to B_\bullet $ is a <em>homotopy equivalence</em> if there exists a morphism $b : B_\bullet \to A_\bullet $ such that there exists a homotopy between $b \circ a$ and $\text{id}_ A$ and there exists a homotopy between $a \circ b$ and $\text{id}_ B$. If there exists such a morphism between $A_\bullet $ and $B_\bullet $, then we say that $A_\bullet $ and $B_\bullet $ are <em>homotopy equivalent</em>. </p>
</article>